<% content_for :title, "Sources and Methodology" %>
<% content_for :description, "How Foundations of the Restoration is researched: its sources, how uncertain dates and estimates are shown, and how conflicting accounts are handled." %>

<%= render "shared/page_header", eyebrow: "How we know", title: "Sources and Methodology",
      lead: "Where the history on this site comes from, how sure we can be of it, and how the site shows the difference." %>

<div class="page grid gap-10 py-10 lg:grid-cols-4">
  <nav class="lg:col-span-1" aria-label="On this page">
    <p class="eyebrow">On this page</p>
    <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-base lg:sticky lg:top-6">
      <% { "approach" => "Our approach", "dates" => "Dates", "figures" => "Figures and estimates", "accounts" => "Facts and interpretation",
           "scope" => "What is included", "images" => "Images", "bibliography" => "Bibliography" }.each do |anchor, label| %>
        <li><%= link_to label, "##{anchor}", class: "text-link font-normal" %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <div class="space-y-12 lg:col-span-3">
    <section id="approach" class="scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Our approach</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <p>
          This site is built on one connected set of records: people, places, events, revelations and the sources behind them.
          The map, the timeline, the charts and the trivia games all read from those same records, so a fact is written once and
          appears the same way everywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every event, place, person and revelation lists the sources that support it at the bottom of its page. Where a source
          is available online, the entry links to it so you can read it yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          Primary sources, written by the people involved or close to the time, are preferred for what happened and when.
          Secondary sources, written later by historians, are used for context and for drawing the story together.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="dates" class="scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Dates</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <p>
          Not every date in early Church history is known to the day. Rather than invent a day or month, each event and
          revelation records how exactly its date is known, and the site shows only what is known.
        </p>
        <table class="w-full text-left text-base">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b-2 border-parchment-300 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">How well the date is known</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">How it is shown</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-semibold">Example</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <% example = Event.date_precision_exact.chronological.first %>
            <tr class="border-b border-parchment-300 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">To the day</th>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">The full date</td>
              <td class="py-2"><% if example %><%= example.display_date %>: <%= link_to example.title, event_path(example), class: "text-link font-normal" %><% end %></td>
            </tr>
            <% { "month" => [ "To the month", "Month and year" ], "year" => [ "To the year", "The year alone" ],
                 "approximate" => [ "Roughly", "The year, marked “c.” (about)" ] }.each do |precision, (label, shown)| %>
              <% example = @date_examples[precision] %>
              <tr class="border-b border-parchment-300 align-top">
                <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold"><%= label %></th>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4"><%= shown %></td>
                <td class="py-2"><% if example %><%= example.display_date %>: <%= link_to example.title, event_path(example), class: "text-link font-normal" %><% end %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          Events with uncertain dates are still placed on the timeline in their most likely order.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="figures" class="scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Figures and estimates</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <p>
          <strong>Membership.</strong> Early membership totals were compiled long afterward from incomplete records. Every
          membership figure on this site is an estimate and is labelled as approximate. They are best read for the overall
          shape of growth, not as exact counts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Counts in charts.</strong> The charts on <%= link_to "Restoration by the Numbers", numbers_path, class: "text-link" %>
          only count things that are complete: the Church’s yearly membership estimates, and all
          <%= DoctrineAndCovenants.sections.size %> sections of the Doctrine and Covenants. They do not count the events, places or
          revelations chosen for this site, because a count of a selection says more about the selection than about history.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Distances.</strong> Distances on the <%= link_to "Road Trip", road_trip_path, class: "text-link" %> are
          straight lines between places. Roads, then and now, are longer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="accounts" class="scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Facts and interpretation</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <p>
          Each entry keeps two things apart. Its summary says what happened, as the sources record it. Its historical context
          explains why it mattered and how it connects to other events, which involves more judgment.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some events are known from more than one account, written at different times and for different readers, and the
          accounts do not always agree in every detail. Where that affects an entry, its context says so instead of choosing
          one version silently.
        </p>
        <% if @uncertain_event %>
          <p>
            Where the sources leave a question open, the entry says that too. For example, no account gives the date of
            <%= link_to @uncertain_event.title, event_path(@uncertain_event), class: "text-link" %>, so it is shown as
            “<%= @uncertain_event.display_date %>” and its entry explains what is and is not known.
          </p>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="scope" class="scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">What is included</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <p>
          The site covers the early Restoration, from the First Vision in 1820 to the arrival of the pioneers in the Salt Lake
          Valley in 1847. It currently holds <%= pluralize(@counts[:events], "event") %>, <%= pluralize(@counts[:places], "place") %>,
          <%= pluralize(@counts[:people], "person") %> and <%= pluralize(@counts[:revelations], "revelation") %>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a selection, chosen to tell the main story, and not a complete record. Leaving something out is not a
          judgment that it was unimportant.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each revelation’s page includes the text of that section of the Doctrine and Covenants, verse by verse. The verse text
          comes from the <%= link_to "Open Scripture API", "https://openscriptureapi.org", class: "text-link", rel: "noopener" %>,
          an independent project. The section headings and footnotes of the current official edition are not reproduced here;
          each page links to the official edition for those.
        </p>
        <p><%= link_to "Browse or search everything in the database", explore_path, class: "text-link" %>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="images" class="scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Images</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <p>
          Historical photographs, documents and maps are used only when they are in the public domain or licensed for reuse.
          Each image is credited where it appears and listed here. Portraits come from Wikimedia Commons, where each is marked
          as being in the public domain. Some are later paintings, drawings or engravings and not likenesses made from life;
          each caption says what the image is.
        </p>
        <% if @images.any? %>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-base">
            <% @images.each do |image| %>
              <li>
                <strong><%= image.caption.presence || image.file_name %></strong>
                <%= [ image.year, image.credit, image.license ].compact_blank.join(" · ") %>
                (<%= link_to record_label(image.imageable), polymorphic_path(image.imageable), class: "text-link font-normal" %><% if image.source_url.present? %>;
                <%= link_to "image source", image.source_url, class: "text-link font-normal", rel: "noopener" %><% end %>)
              </li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
        <% else %>
          <p class="text-slate-600">No images have been added yet.</p>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="bibliography" class="scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Bibliography</h2>
      <% { "Primary sources" => @primary, "Secondary sources" => @secondary }.each do |heading, sources| %>
        <% next if sources.empty? %>
        <h3 class="mt-6 text-2xl font-bold"><%= heading %></h3>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-4">
          <% sources.each do |source| %>
            <li class="card p-5">
              <h4 class="font-serif text-xl font-bold text-forest-900"><%= source.title %></h4>
              <% if source.author.present? %><p class="text-base text-brown-700"><%= source.author %></p><% end %>
              <% if source.citation.present? %><p class="mt-2 text-base"><%= source.citation %></p><% end %>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base">
                <% if source.url.present? %>
                  <%= link_to "Visit source", source.url, class: "text-link", rel: "noopener" %> ·
                <% end %>
                <span class="text-slate-600">Cited by <%= pluralize(source.citations.size, "entry") %></span>
              </p>
            </li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      <% end %>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
